<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mayo College</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1875">1875</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mayo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mayo College is a boys-only private boarding school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded in 1875 and named after the 6th Earl of Mayo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a> from 1869 to 1872. Proposed in 1869 by Colonel Walter, it was meant to be an Eton of India: the British built it for the sons of the Indian upper classes, particularly the princes and nobles, to give the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> an education like that of Eton College. Its first student, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Mangal Singh of Alwar, arrived in October 1875 on an elephant with 300 retainers. Its coat of arms, designed by Lockwood Kipling, carries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> emblems, and it has educated many members of India's royal families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mayo-college.jpg" alt="Mayo College" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Singh92karan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mayo_college_in_a_sunny_day.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
